      <h2 id="experience-heading">Choose a cabin for how you'll use it.</h2>

      <h3>Traveling with teenagers</h3>
      <p>Connecting cabins can be reassuring, but two balconies can stretch the budget. If the adults want a balcony and the teenagers won't use one much, an interior cabin directly across the hall is another option to compare. I check the travelers' ages, exact cabin locations, and Royal Caribbean's booking rules before recommending that arrangement.</p>

      <h3>What worked for us on Allure</h3>
      <p>On Allure of the Seas, we chose connecting Central Park View Interior cabins with windows overlooking the park. They were enough for our family because we spent more time enjoying the ship than sitting in our rooms.</p>

      <h3>Space matters, too</h3>
      <p>On Utopia of the Seas, our cabin felt less cramped. That's part of the comparison I help you make: the room's layout, what it costs, and how much time you'll actually spend there.</p>

      <p class="about__takeaway">It's cost versus utility. During our initial call, I'll help you decide where a balcony, extra space, or connecting door is worth the spend for your group.</p>
